## 11. Memory Retrieval — When to Recall

Recalling on every turn is wasteful. Most turns need no long-term memory at all.

```python
async def should_recall(query: str, recent: list[Message]) -> bool:
    """A cheap gate before an expensive retrieval."""
    # 1. Explicit reference to the past — always recall.
    if re.search(r"\b(earlier|before|last time|previously|you said|we discussed|"
                 r"remember|as I mentioned)\b", query, re.I):
        return True
    # 2. First turn — nothing to recall.
    if len(recent) <= 2:
        return False
    # 3. Pronouns with no local antecedent suggest older context.
    if re.match(r"^(it|that|this|those|they)\b", query.strip(), re.I) and len(recent) < 4:
        return True
    # 4. Short follow-ups usually refer to the immediately preceding turn.
    if len(query.split()) < 5:
        return False
    return False        # default: don't. Recency handles most conversation.
```

**Retrieval quality rules:**
```
 ✔ Filter by conversation AND tenant INSIDE the vector search, never after
 ✔ Re-sort retrieved turns chronologically before injecting
 ✔ Include the adjacent turn — a lone reply is often meaningless
 ✔ Label them clearly: "From an earlier conversation on 2026-02-14:"
   (unlabelled old turns get mistaken for current context)
 ✔ Set a similarity floor — a weak match is worse than nothing
 ✔ Deduplicate against what's already in the recent window
```

