## 12. Persistent Memory — Storage

```python
# Postgres = the source of truth
class Message(Base):
    id, conversation_id, org_id, role, content, tokens, created_at
    # append-only; never mutated

class ConversationMemory(Base):
    conversation_id (pk), summary, last_summarised_index, updated_at
    # a CACHE derived from messages — safely rebuildable

class UserFact(Base):
    id, user_id, org_id, subject, predicate, object, confidence
    source_message_id, created_at, superseded_at, superseded_by
    # never hard-delete — supersede, so history is auditable

# Vector store = the retrieval index
#   collection per tenant; payload carries org_id, conv_id, ts, role
```

**Rules:**
```
 ✔ Raw messages are the source of truth; summaries and vectors are derived
   and rebuildable. Never let a summary be the only copy of something.
 ✔ Facts are superseded, not deleted — you need the history for audit
   and for "why does it think that about me?"
 ✔ Every memory read is tenant-scoped. Memory is a data-leak surface
   exactly like RAG.
 ✔ Retention: define it, honour it, and make deletion actually delete —
   including vectors and derived summaries.
 ✔ Users must be able to VIEW and DELETE what the system remembers about
   them. This is a GDPR/CCPA requirement, not a nice-to-have.
```

